function calculatePi(iterations) {
  let pi = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < iterations; i++) {
    const sign = Math.pow(-1, i);
    const term = 4 / (2 * i + 1);
    pi += sign * term;
  }
  return pi;
}

const iterations = 1000000; // You can adjust the number of iterations as needed
const calculatedPi = calculatePi(iterations);
console.log("Calculated Pi:", calculatedPi);
```

This code uses the Leibniz formula, which is an infinite series that converges to π/4. The more iterations you use, the closer the approximation will be to the actual value of π.

Note: This method is not very efficient and may take some time to compute with a large number of iterations. There are other algorithms available that converge faster, such as the Gauss-Legendre algorithm or the Chudnovsky algorithm. However, these require more complex mathematical operations and are beyond the scope of this simple example.

